"""Preparation-only reference-as-solution for ``aten::indices`` (operator 2162).

The entrypoint ``run`` is the declared ABI of the injected candidate
(``--override indices:<this file>:run``), so it takes exactly one positional
operand and returns the native ATen result.

Why the native op is the faithful oracle here:

* ``aten::indices(Tensor(a) self) -> Tensor(a)`` is a sparse COO metadata
  accessor. Its result is the operand's stored index tensor itself, so the
  returned object inherits the operand's strides and storage offset and shares
  the coordinate storage (writes through the result land in the operand and a
  later call sees them). The native call is the only oracle that reproduces
  that identity, the int64 promotion and the ``(sparse_dim, nnz)`` shape for
  hybrid operands without re-implementing any of it here.
* The operator's rejection contract is caller-visible and byte-exact: an
  uncoalesced COO operand, a dense/strided operand and a CSR/CSC operand are
  all refused with the native ``RuntimeError`` text, a non-tensor operand and a
  missing operand raise at call binding. Delegating keeps those messages and
  error types native instead of retyping them, which is what the frozen tests
  compare against ``torch.ops.aten.indices`` element-by-element.

It imports no ``flag_gems`` symbol and never calls ``flag_gems.indices``, so it
is not its own oracle and cannot recurse into the rebound export while the
override is installed. It is a preparation tool for the review chain, not an
optimised candidate: it performs no computation of its own and claims nothing
about candidate numerics or performance.
"""

import torch


def run(self):
    """Return ``torch.ops.aten.indices(self)`` (native oracle, unchanged)."""
    return torch.ops.aten.indices(self)
